{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792447683,"nonce":"WpnkxviEa0IbqdkBjI4x8PpcgwY=","message":"Cap editor row pools at the visible source length\n\nCodeEditor/CodeView allocated a whole viewport's slots even for empty or very short sources. Each hidden slot still created reactive state, gutter controls and handlers. Cap capacity at max(1, min(visible document lines, viewport plus overscan)); scrolling long sources still reuses its bounded slots. Also clamp offsets during layout when source replacement or folding makes the content smaller, preventing the view remaining beyond the end.\n\nThe temporary Haxe-only prototype in RayDE's same dark ten-tab/Git workload reduced median total allocated per code-tab switch from 38.86 MiB to 12.59 MiB and median three-frame work from 150.7 ms to 59.3 ms. Physical footprint after closing/collecting/draining fell from 327.9 to 287.9 MiB; this is a sample, not a runtime-memory guarantee. Artifacts live in RayDE bin/interaction-row-cap-trial alongside the prior bin/interaction-document-state run.\n\nCodeComponents now passes 274 checks (both schemes), including short editor and read-only pools, folded rows with no missing line numbers, long-document slot reuse, and shrinking a scrolled source to one correctly rendered row. CodeStateLifecycle passes all 20 checks for document identity, undo/redo, selection/folds/scroll persistence and disposal. Native sources are unchanged.","files":null}]}